      "settings": {
        "title": "Baking Knowledge Q&A",
        "flour_q1": "Question 01: If I don't have the required gluten strength flour at home, how can I mix it myself?",
        "flour_a1": "1:1 mixture of high gluten flour and low gluten flour equals medium gluten flour;\nHigh gluten flour + cornstarch 4:1 mixture = medium gluten flour;\nMedium gluten flour + cornstarch 4:1 mixture = low gluten flour;\nHigh gluten flour + cornstarch 1:1 mixture = low gluten flour\nMedium gluten flour can also increase gluten strength by adding vital wheat gluten, but the specific ratio will vary depending on different brands.",
        "flour_q2": "Question 02: What are the main classifications of flour?",
        "flour_a2": "The most commonly used classification currently is still based on protein content, mainly divided into three categories: high gluten flour, medium gluten flour, and low gluten flour.",
        "flour_q3": "Question 03: What is low gluten flour?",
        "flour_a3": "Low gluten flour is also called cake flour. Mainly used for making various types of cakes, pastries, etc. Its protein content ranges from 6.5% to 8.5%. Color pure white, easy to knead into balls, easy to shape.",
        "flour_q4": "Question 04: What is medium gluten flour?",
        "flour_a4": "Medium gluten flour is what we call regular flour. The protein content ranges from 8.0% to 10.5%. It is suitable for making steamed buns, dumplings, pancakes and other Chinese pastries. Its color is milky white, semi-loose, with balanced gluten strength and viscosity.",
        "flour_q5": "Question 05: What is high gluten flour?",
        "flour_a5": "The protein content is 11.5%~14.5%. It has strong gluten, high viscosity, and is suitable for making bread, noodles, etc. It has a darker color and smooth texture. When you grab a handful of high gluten flour and squeeze it tightly with your fist, then release it, it will quickly fall apart and not easily form a dough.",
        "egg_q1": "Question 01: What is the typical size of eggs mentioned in baking recipes?",
        "egg_a1": "Choose eggs with a shell weight of about 60 grams for baking.\nThe optimal storage temperature for eggs is 2-6°C. When making butter cookies, it's recommended to take the eggs out in advance to return to room temperature.",
        "milk_q1": "Question 02: What kind of milk is used in baking?",
        "milk_a1": "The milk in baking recipes refers to \"whole milk\", which can enhance the flavor of baked goods, making them moist, smooth, and rich in milky aroma.",
        "butter_q1": "Question 003: What are the classifications of butter in baking?",
        "butter_a1": "Butter comes in salted and unsalted varieties. Generally, unsalted butter is used in baking. If salted butter is used, the amount of salt in the recipe needs to be reduced accordingly. However, since the salt content in different salted butters is not consistent, it is not recommended to use salted butter in baking.\nIn addition, butter has another classification: animal butter and plant butter. From a health and taste perspective, animal butter is more recommended.",
        "oil_q1": "Question 004: What oils are referred to in cake recipes?",
        "oil_a1": "Oils that are colorless and odorless generally refer to salad oil/corn oil, while peanut oil is generally used for making Cantonese mooncakes.",
        "butter_q2": "Question 006: What is butter?",
        "butter_a2": "It is the fat extracted from milk, so in some places it is also called \"ghee\". Butter contains about 80% fat, and the rest is water and other milk components, possessing natural rich milky aroma.",
        "butter_q3": "Question 007: Is butter the same as cream?",
        "butter_a3": "Butter is called cream in China's national standards. Butter is our common term. Many people think that \"cream\" refers to the cream used for frosting, like on birthday cakes. In fact, the \"cream\" used for frosting is called \"light cream\" in national standards, English name is Cream, and the product name is generally \"heavy cream\". So cream and butter are the same thing, referring to butter. And light cream (or light cream) is the cream for frosting.",
        "butter_q4": "Question 008: What are the conditions for butter to be suitable for whipping?",
        "butter_a4": "Butter is relatively hard in refrigerated state, but becomes very soft at around 28°C, at which point it can be whipped by stirring to incorporate air, making the volume larger, commonly known as \"creaming\". When the temperature exceeds 34°C, the butter will melt into liquid form. It should be noted that butter can only be whipped when it is soft, and cannot be whipped after melting. In winter when the temperature is low, it can be softened by short microwave heating.",
        "cream_q1": "Question 009: How to whip heavy cream?",
        "cream_a1": "To whip heavy cream, first select a variety that can be whipped, that is, heavy cream with the English name whipping cream.\nFirst, heavy cream must be whipped in a refrigerated state. Before whipping heavy cream, refrigerate it for more than 12 hours.\nSecond, add sugar to the heavy cream and use an electric mixer at medium speed. If making decorated cakes, when the heavy cream is whipped to a fluffy volume that can maintain patterns, it can be used.\nThird, be careful when whipping heavy cream not to use too high a speed, especially when whipping small amounts of heavy cream. If over-whipped, the heavy cream will appear like cottage cheese and cannot be used.",
        "cream_q2": "Question 010: What is the ratio of heavy cream to sugar?",
        "cream_a2": "Heavy cream itself is not sweet, so sugar needs to be added before whipping according to personal taste. The commonly used ratio of heavy cream to sugar is generally 10:1, but can be increased or decreased according to preferred sweetness.",
        "cream_q3": "Question 011: How to store animal heavy cream?",
        "cream_a3": "It can only be stored refrigerated, never frozen, otherwise oil-water separation will occur after thawing. Frozen light cream can be used for bread ingredients, but cannot be whipped.",
        "sugar_q1": "Question 012: What are the classifications of sugar in baking?",
        "sugar_a1": "Cake sugar: granulated sugar, powdered sugar, brown sugar; Pastry sugar: brown sugar, yellow sugar; Decorative sugar: coarse sugar, moisture-proof powdered sugar. \"Fine granulated sugar\" has fine particles, easy to mix and dissolve evenly.",
        "sugar_q2": "Question 013: What are the functions of sugar in baking?",
        "sugar_a2": "First, it helps with coloring. The golden color produced after baking comes from the caramelization of sugar, such as the common caramel products. The higher the sugar content in the recipe, the easier it is to develop color during baking.",
        "sugar_q3": "Question 014: What is maltose used for?",
        "sugar_a3": "Maltose, also called malt sugar, is made from grains such as corn, barley, wheat, and chestnuts through fermentation and saccharification. It is generally a viscous and translucent syrup. Maltose is commonly used to make toffee, Shaqima, etc. Using maltose to make pastries not only adds flavor, but also improves the internal structure of the pastries, making the finished product more fluffy and delicious, and easier to bake beautiful colors.",
        "tool_q1": "Question 029: Does the oven need to be preheated? How long in advance?",
        "tool_a1": "Yes. Preheating is required for baking any desserts or pastries. It is recommended to preheat the oven 10 minutes in advance. Currently, there are some ovens on the market that can achieve no preheating.",
        "tool_q2": "Question 030: Why does cake flour need to be sifted?",
        "tool_a2": "Cake flour has hygroscopic properties, and small lumps may appear in the flour. Unsifted flour is prone to forming \"flour lumps\" when mixed with water, milk and other wet ingredients, resulting in uneven batter.",
        "tool_q3": "Question 032: What to do if the home oven doesn't have separate top and bottom heating?",
        "tool_a3": "Just add the required top and bottom fire temperatures in the recipe and divide by 2, then place it in the appropriate oven position based on the size comparison of top and bottom fire temperatures. For example: If the original recipe is top fire 200°C/bottom fire 180°C, if only a general oven is available, just set the temperature to (200+180)/2=190°C."
      }
